and we are all being cautioned to limit  our interactions for the thanksgiving  holiday to try to really reduce the  spread of covet 19.  of course it is a very different holiday  this year so the experts are  recommending different things one thing  is to keep those interactions  essentially in our bubble our immediate  family if you will  but in some other cultures social  bubbles are encouraged now kirsten joyce  is live in studio  explaining more on how to essentially  kirsten bubble safely  that's right brian but first let's be  clear here when you interact with more  people you do increase your odds of  acquiring and spreading covet 19.  staying home and limiting your  face-to-face contact with people  is still the best way to avoid it but in  new zealand and parts of the uk  social bubbles are encouraged they can  be a very effective way of extending  contact beyond the household if managed  properly  because you commit to only hanging out  with certain individuals all right  this might be what many of you are  already doing you probably already have  your  covet 19 bubble it could be your group  of close friends perhaps  extended family neighbors even your  co-workers but keep in mind  bubbles can also make it a little  difficult to perform contact tracing  so here is how to bubble safely there we  go know about people's health conditions  ask your friends or potential bubble  mates if they have any underlying health  conditions that  could make them more susceptible to  getting covet 19  set some ground rules have a  conversation about what the expectations  are for the group  and know what precautions people have  been taking and one last note feel free  not to bubble even if your best friend  in the world the cdc says  you shouldn't get together with people  who have not been wearing masks or  practicing good hygiene
 

